

<% content_for :title, "Stoicism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .stoicism-bipolar-page .stoicism-copy,
  .stoicism-bipolar-page .stoicism-values > div,
  .stoicism-bipolar-page .stoicism-practice-list > div {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .stoicism-bipolar-page .stoicism-source {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.75em;
    font-style: normal;
  }
</style>

<main class="stoicism-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="stoicism-bipolar-hero">
    <div class="stoicism-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="stoicism-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / STOICISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>STOIC LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="stoicism-bipolar-intro">
        YOU CAN'T CONTROL EVERYTHING.<br>
        FOCUS ON WHAT YOU CAN INFLUENCE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-pink" aria-labelledby="stoicism-section-1-title">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        01 / THE DICHOTOMY OF CONTROL
      </p>

      <h2 id="stoicism-section-1-title">
        WHAT'S<br>
        ACTUALLY<br>
        YOURS?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <blockquote>
        <p>“Of things some are in our power, and others are not.”</p>
        <cite class="stoicism-source">— Epictetus</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Stoicism asks you to distinguish what you can influence from what you cannot.
        You cannot will away an episode, change the past, or control other people’s
        judgments. You can support stability through treatment, routines, recognizing
        warning signs, and asking for help.
      </p>

      <p>
        Severe symptoms can impair judgment and self-control. Preparing during stability
        gives you support when independent decision-making becomes harder.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-yellow" aria-labelledby="stoicism-section-2-title">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        02 / IMPRESSIONS
      </p>

      <h2 id="stoicism-section-2-title">
        FIRST THOUGHT<br>
        ≠ FACT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p>
        Your first judgment is not necessarily the full picture. Returning symptoms may
        feel like proof that all your progress is gone. A setback does not erase what
        you have accomplished. Approach it as a challenge: what resources and useful
        actions are available now?
      </p>

      <p>
        Reframing means examining the meaning you add to an experience while
        acknowledging the suffering that is already there.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-black" aria-labelledby="stoicism-section-3-title">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        03 / RESPONSE
      </p>

      <h2 id="stoicism-section-3-title">
        PUT SPACE<br>
        BEFORE<br>
        ACTION.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p>
        Anger, cravings, and urgency can push you toward confrontation, spending, or
        substance use. When you can, pause and check whether your interpretation is
        accurate and the action supports your long-term well-being. Self-discipline
        means choosing deliberately where choice is available; emotions themselves do
        not need to disappear.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-purple" aria-labelledby="stoicism-section-4-title">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        04 / ACCEPTANCE
      </p>

      <h2 id="stoicism-section-4-title">
        REALITY<br>
        ALREADY<br>
        HAPPENED.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p>
        During mania, confidence can make decisions feel unquestionably right. During
        depression, hopelessness can make your efforts feel worthless. Mixed symptoms
        may make deliberate responses especially difficult.
      </p>

      <p>
        Accept those limits and rely on your treatment plan and trusted support when
        needed. Your worth is not measured by productivity, happiness, or how
        independently you manage symptoms.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-pink" aria-labelledby="stoicism-section-5-title">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        05 / CHARACTER
      </p>

      <h2 id="stoicism-section-5-title">
        WHO DO<br>
        YOU WANT<br>
        TO BE?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p>
        Four virtues can give your choices direction:
      </p>

      <div class="stoicism-values">

        <div>
          <span>WISDOM</span>
          <p>Recognize limits and examine your judgments.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>COURAGE</span>
          <p>Face difficulty and accept help.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>JUSTICE</span>
          <p>Take responsibility and repair harm.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>TEMPERANCE</span>
          <p>Practice moderation where you can.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        You cannot guarantee forgiveness or undo harm. Make amends where possible and
        work toward different decisions. Regret can guide growth without becoming
        endless self-punishment.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-yellow" aria-labelledby="stoicism-section-6-title">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        06 / THE OBSTACLE
      </p>

      <h2 id="stoicism-section-6-title">
        USE THE<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        THING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <p>
        Negative visualization briefly considers losing something you value to deepen
        appreciation for it. The aim is gratitude for stability, relationships, and
        ordinary peace, rather than dwelling on disaster.
      </p>

      <p>
        Resilience does not require unnecessary suffering, lost sleep, or pushing past
        your limits. Rest, adjusted expectations, and support can be courageous
        responses.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 -->
  <section class="stoicism-section stoicism-black" aria-labelledby="stoicism-section-7-title">

    <div class="stoicism-section-heading">
      <p class="stoicism-number">
        07 / PUT IT INTO PRACTICE
      </p>

      <h2 id="stoicism-section-7-title">
        OKAY.<br>
        NOW WHAT?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-copy">

      <div class="stoicism-practice-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>
            <strong>IDENTIFY THE FACTS.</strong><br>
            What happened before I added a judgment?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>
            <strong>CHECK CONTROL.</strong><br>
            What can I influence today?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>
            <strong>QUESTION THE THOUGHT.</strong><br>
            What evidence supports this first interpretation?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>
            <strong>PAUSE.</strong><br>
            Where possible, delay acting on urgency.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>
            <strong>CHECK YOUR VALUES.</strong><br>
            What would wisdom, courage, fairness, or moderation suggest?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>06</span>
          <p>
            <strong>ACCEPT HELP.</strong><br>
            Follow your care plan and ask for support.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="stoicism-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="stoicism-ending-small">
        YOU DON'T CONTROL THE WEATHER.
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOU STEER<br>
        <span>THE SHIP.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="stoicism-ending-copy">

      <p>
        You do not have to turn every painful experience into something positive or
        endure it alone. Keep practicing resilience with compassion, accepting reality
        while leaving room for change. Use whatever wisdom, support, and resources are
        available to you.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "stoicism-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>


